course: dictionary
lesson: 5359
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. King is one of them. It can mean a man who rules a country, a special piece or card in a game, or anything that is the best or most powerful of its kind.
explain: «Charles became King the moment his mother died.» Here king means a ruler, because a person becomes king when a parent who was king dies. Ask yourself: which meaning fits this sentence? Then try it in the sentence to check.
explain: «The lion is the king of the jungle.» No real lion is a ruler of a country, so this king must mean the most powerful one of its kind. A good clue is to ask whether the meaning makes sense in the real world.
explain: When you meet a word you know, do not stop too early. Look at the words around it, check which meaning fits, and you will understand the sentence much better.
fact: A king is a man who rules a country, often because his parent was king or queen before him.
fact: In a card game or in chess, the king is a special piece or card, and in chess it is the piece you must protect.
fact: «The King says the Cat may kiss his hand» comes from a storybook, where the King is a character.
fact: Today the United Kingdom, Canada, Australia and New Zealand have a king as head of state, but he does not make the laws.
fact: Magna Carta in 1215 set the idea that even the king must follow the law.
exercise: «Charles became King the moment his mother died.» What does king mean here? | The best of its kind, like a lion | A place where a queen lives | A high card in a game | *A man who rules a country
exercise: In chess, which piece must you protect most? | Pawn | Bishop | Rook | *King
exercise: «The lion is the king of the jungle.» What does king mean here? | A castle on a hill | A playing card with a picture on it | *The most powerful one of its kind | A man who rules a country
test: Which country has a king as head of state today, but he does not make the laws? | *United Kingdom | Egypt | Germany | France
test: Magna Carta in 1215 set the idea that what? | *Even the king must follow the law | Kings should own all the land | Kings must be crowned in Westminster Abbey | Castles must have a moat
exam: Jayden says king only means a man who rules a country. Is he right? | No, king means a queen | Yes, because kings always live in castles | Yes, king never has any other meaning | *No, king can also mean a playing card, a chess piece, or the best of its kind
